The advantage of LiteLLM is to allow for the same model call to be applied to various LLMs from different sources
Model requests are sent to a single API (Ollama)
Avoids requests being sent to OpenAI's cloud servers ("don't go out to the Internet for this, talk to my machine instead")

completion() is a wrapper function that routes the request to the right place, based on the prefix of the model string;
The 'ollama_chat/' prefix points it towards Ollama chat request formatting
It also normalizes the response into one consistent shape (OpenAI's response format)
"LiteLLM translates whatever format the real backend returned into that one shape before handing it back to you."


Although model call is the same, we are not expecting the same answer - 
LiteLLM only standardizes the calling code (the shape of the function call/ request / response format)
Model response depends on what each model knows, how it reasons and how it phrases things
The decoupling between call and response - same code, swappable model - is the selling point of the library

Utility:
**Benchmarking:** identical calling code (only model string changes) keeps model comparison honest and avoids bias
introduced by subtle differences on prompt formatting, handling retries or parse responses
**Combining multiple models in one agentic system:** a production agent often routes different sub-tasks to different models
according to cost/capability; with LiteLLM you can use different model backbones without having to design a specific model call for each
**Avoid vendor lock-in:** switching SDK provider requires rewriting calling code; LiteLLM avoids this altogether (only requires a config change)
**Production features:** automatic fallback if a provider is down or rate-limited; load-balancing across API keys; cost tracking

